+### Cutter Geometry Rendering
+**Definition**: Rendering occurs only inside the selected geometric object's volume. If Fire Lights are enabled, only those inside the cutter are rendered. Parameters: usegizmo (enable), gizmo (object) [S4].
+**Parameters**: Invert Cutter (invgizmo): When enabled, rendering occurs only outside the render cutter. Rays not intersecting the cutter are shaded as well. Distinct from cutter with inverted geometry [S4].
+**Pitfalls**: Cutter Geom does not work in Volumetric Geometry mode [S4].
 
 ## Background
-The technique was popularized in the early 2000s as graphics hardware evolved to support 3D textures and hardware alpha blending. Key early works include Lefohn et al. (2004) for on-the-fly slice reconstruction, Rezk-Salama et al. (2000) for trilinear interpolation in fragment shaders, and Kniss et al. (2002b) for addressing intensity variations and popping artifacts. The method is widely used in medical imaging and scientific visualization where interactive frame rates are required for large datasets.
+The technique originated with the advent of 3D texture support in graphics hardware, allowing volumetric data to be stored directly in GPU memory [S1][S2, unofficial]. Early implementations used object-aligned slicing, where the volume was sliced along the principal axes (x, y, z) [S2, unofficial]. This approach was fast but suffered from memory overhead (requiring replication of data in three directions) and view-dependent artifacts such as intensity variations and image popping when switching between texture stacks [S2, unofficial]. View-aligned slicing was introduced to address these issues by dynamically generating slice geometry aligned with the camera view, ensuring consistent sampling distance and reducing artifacts, albeit at the cost of increased CPU/GPU geometry generation overhead [S2, unofficial]. The method has been widely used in medical imaging and scientific visualization for interactive exploration of large datasets [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
